 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Owner-occupiers are paying about **6.20%** on new variable-rate loans, roughly **2.6 percentage points below** the advertised standard variable rate of **8.77%**.\n\n| Measure | Rate | Period |\n|---|---|---|\n| Advertised standard variable rate (owner-occupier) | 8.77% | September 2026 (RBA F5) |\n| Advertised discounted variable rate (owner-occupier) | 6.80% | September 2026 (RBA F5) |\n| **Average rate on new owner-occupier variable loans, all institutions** | **6.20%** | **August 2026 (RBA F6)** |\n| Average rate on new owner-occupier variable loans, large institutions | 6.20% | August 2026 (RBA F6) |\n\nThe gap is wide because most borrowers negotiate a discount off the advertised rate. The 6.80% discounted rate is still above what new borrowers actually average, so the typical discount is larger than the headline discount figure suggests.\n\nOver the past year, the average new-loan rate rose from 5.5% (September 2025 to January 2026) to 6.2% (May to August 2026). Over the same period the advertised standard rate went from 8.02% to 8.77%. The advertised rate has moved more, so the gap has narrowed slightly in percentage terms but has stayed large.\n\nThe F6 figures are published with a lag, so August 2026 is the latest available for actual new-loan rates.",
